    [
     1,
     "Hi,\r<br>\r<br>you have to extract thee correct elements of the output from\r<br>inla.posterior.sample, and I&#39;m not sure $latent is what you want, since\r<br>you also compare (it seems) with the fitted values.\r<br>\r<br>What do you really want?  joint samples of the linear predictor and then\r<br>from these new samples of the poisson data?\r<br>\r<br>Best\r<br>H\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Wed, 2014-08-13 at 05:59 -0700, Rachel wrote:\r<br>&gt; Dear R-inla\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I have a Poisson model for counts of cases. I fit the model using\r<br>&gt; INLA. I would then like to obtain samples of the joint posterior (at\r<br>&gt; the response scale). \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; However, the output does not look anything like the fitted values\r<br>&gt; obtained using \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; result$summary.fitted.values$mean\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; head(result$summary.fitted.values$mean)\r<br>&gt; [1] 174.2283 212.1516 248.9024 185.8098 182.7909 205.3810\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Here is the code:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; result=inla(formula,data=test,family=&quot;poisson&quot;, offset=log(E), verbose\r<br>&gt; =\r<br>&gt; T,control.compute=list(dic=TRUE,config=TRUE),control.predictor=list(link = 1,compute=TRUE))\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; k=10\r<br>&gt; samples = inla.posterior.sample(k,result)\r<br>&gt; samples.m = t(sapply(samples, function(x) x$latent)) \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; samples.m[1:10,1:10]\r<br>&gt;            [,1]      [,2]      [,3]      [,4]      [,5]      [,6]\r<br>&gt; [,7]      [,8]      [,9]     [,10]\r<br>&gt;  [1,] -4.583270 -4.565974 -4.533882 -4.560919 -4.565828 -4.541501\r<br>&gt; -4.568346 -4.561320 -4.605616 -4.555934\r<br>&gt;  [2,] -4.594975 -4.582343 -4.543172 -4.558481 -4.568398 -4.546659\r<br>&gt; -4.582610 -4.566035 -4.603038 -4.551326\r<br>&gt;  [3,] -4.594717 -4.569229 -4.541903 -4.557828 -4.564072 -4.537166\r<br>&gt; -4.579652 -4.559855 -4.605235 -4.545860\r<br>&gt;  [4,] -4.597460 -4.569987 -4.548202 -4.569006 -4.563227 -4.539672\r<br>&gt; -4.577936 -4.567094 -4.603760 -4.555862\r<br>&gt;  [5,] -4.587566 -4.575019 -4.539236 -4.563575 -4.562490 -4.535971\r<br>&gt; -4.572190 -4.571462 -4.604253 -4.551189\r<br>&gt;  [6,] -4.589880 -4.575138 -4.538666 -4.561069 -4.570671 -4.543182\r<br>&gt; -4.577292 -4.558420 -4.598595 -4.550528\r<br>&gt;  [7,] -4.590320 -4.568199 -4.542620 -4.565529 -4.560840 -4.538337\r<br>&gt; -4.577035 -4.561788 